// Calling a gRPC service while the program runs, and the marks that say which
// protobuf message each struct is.
//
//	#[grpc.message("library.v1.Book")]
//	struct Book {
//	    #[grpc.field(1)] id int
//	    #[grpc.field(2)] title string
//	}
//
//	match var r = grpc.call[GetBookRequest, Book](server="localhost:50051", method="/library.v1.Library/GetBook", request={id=7}) {
//	    ok { … r.title … }
//	    failed { … grpc.classify(r).kind … }
//	}
//
// A program rarely writes either: `import api "grpc:./library.proto"` writes
// the structs, the enums and a function per method.
//
// **The marks are the schema.** A field's number and kind, a member's number
// and a struct's message name land on the IR (`ir.CodecProto`), so each
// language writes the bytes itself from the plan -- straight into its own
// types, with no message object between -- or hands `descriptor` to protoc's
// own generation.
//
// A call waits, as any async function does; `stream` calls a streaming
// method, handing each response to a function as it comes. Metadata,
// deadlines and credentials are not set yet.
